WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.630
I think so many technology companies are
trying to solve problems of people who

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:08.190
live in a few specific cities of this
particular country. When in reality,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.480
building the future that we want or
that we'd be proud to hand to our kids,

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.360
involves humanity solving
problems at global scale.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.270
This is Zipline Drone Delivery.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.220
The startup founded in 2014 helped build
hype for a future filled with packages

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:37.080
falling conveniently from the sky. As
that future becomes more of a reality,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.650
Zipline will have to compete with
the likes of Amazon's Prime Air.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:41.610
Let me show you something.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.630
Oh my God.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.110
Remember when Jeff Bezos unveiled
an octocopter on 60 Minutes?

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.580
These are octocopters.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.520
That was a decade ago. No octocopters yet,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.380
but Amazon is making slow progress
and Alphabet's Wing is getting off the

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:56.213
ground as well.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.280
Hello? Hey, thank you for
coming. Good to see you. No,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.860
it's good to see you again. It's
been a while. Welcome to Zipline.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.020
We serve eight countries. Today,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.980
we serve about 3,500 hospitals
and health facilities globally.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.530
It's become the largest
commercial-autonomous system on earth.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.150
Zipline honed its technology
first in Rwanda, delivering blood,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.560
vaccines and other medical supplies,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.760
especially to rural areas that were
previously poorly served by land.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.450
Does that give you chills that you
guys are out there saving lives?

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.590
It definitely never gets old,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.160
especially when we get to
go spend time with doctors,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.930
meet patients who are
benefiting from the system,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.540
and it's been really cool
to see the system scale.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.910
Can you show us how the magic
happens? Yeah, let's do it.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.750
So this is Zipline's main manufacturing
area. Zipline has two products today.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.100
Platform 1 is really focused on
enterprise delivery. Platform 2,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.960
we're launching with
customers in a few months.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.270
So that's the one that's going to be
delivering all the packages to my door?

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:57.930
 Yep,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.050
exactly. We're a pretty
full-stack company.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.960
We're building LRUs or
line replaceable units.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.700
So basically the
sub-assemblies of the aircraft.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:06.510
It all happens here?

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.260
Yeah. You can think of this
as the ears of the aircraft.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.020
So we're passing a huge
amount of air through here.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.930
It's like a really quiet wind tunnel.
This is the paraland tube.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.700
If we're encountering such insane
weather conditions or there is systematic

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.790
failure throughout the entire aircraft
and across multiple levels of redundancy,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.880
then we can deploy the parachute and
we can bring the aircraft gently to the

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.750
ground. This is the reason that through
50 million commercial-autonomous miles,

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.320
Zipline has had zero human-safety
incidents. See these two holes?

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.720
You're going to line those up with
those two. I think I can do this. Nice.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.000
You did it. Good job.
Oh. Not heavy at all.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.680
Yeah, or you're really strong.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.850
This is the advantage of
carbon fiber materials.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.040
You can design something
that's really strong.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.560
It's designed to go up
to about 3G's of load.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.810
Where we are now, we call it Nest Zero.
This is actually the first test site.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:07.150
What's the grand plan?

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.120
When we started building Zipline,
the more we thought about logistics,

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.270
the more we realized global logistics
really only does a good job of serving

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.260
people in the golden billion. So
the richest billion people on earth,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.060
your access is probably OK.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:19.750
But.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.000
For the 7 billion people who
don't fall into that category,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.980
your access either
sucks or is nonexistent.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.173
And.

00:03:25.727 --> 00:03:26.560
 As a result of that,

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.950
5.5 million kids under the age of five
lose their lives every year due to lack

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.120
of access to basic medical products.
From a mission perspective,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.900
what's exciting to us about building an
automated-logistics system is that we'll

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.260
be the first logistics system
that serves all people equally.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.990
It will enable us to transition
logistics to zero emission.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.410
It'll also enable universal access
to a lot of different products,

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:48.580
including lifesaving products.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.320
So not to overly simplify it,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:54.100
but is the goal to be like
a modern day UPS or FedEx?

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.560
Or is this something bigger?

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.480
I actually think the opportunity to
build a system that is 10 times faster,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:03.970
half the cost, zero emission,

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:09.070
and broadly available to every
human on Earth is probably like 10

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.940
times or larger. Emily, this is Fabiola.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:13.750
Hi. I started.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.970
At Zipline four years
and a few months ago.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.440
And it was Rwanda One. Yes.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.040
It's basically members of the Rwanda
and Ghana flight operations teams now

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.950
having to train all of our US
team members on how to do this at

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.950
scale.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.800
Three, two, one...

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.800
Alright.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.370
That's amazing.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.680
A ton of t-shirts. Oh,
t-shirts, guys. And water. So.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:57.580
Prepared.

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:02.410
So is the goal to deliver
everything from blood to vaccines

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.480
to my Kleenex and sushi?

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:08.650
I mean, it turns out that our
customers want everything. In the US,

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:10.000
a good analogy with Walmart,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.150
we had initially planned to launch with
them around their pharmacy business.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.860
They're one of the largest
pharmacies in the US.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.930
We launched delivering
healthcare products,

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.900
but very rapidly it was clear that
customers wanted to order everything.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.940
And so today we deliver 24,000
of the 29,000 SKUs in the

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.290
store that we're attached to.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:30.280
You do have competition, Amazon
Prime Air, Alphabet's Wing.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.610
What makes you think you
can lead this market?

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:37.390
I think there are a lot of big companies
with kind of infinite resources talking

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.840
about this market, but talk
and walk are different things.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.470
We are the largest commercial-autonomous
system on earth of any kind,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:45.880
ground or air. So.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:46.990
Compared to Amazon? You know...

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:53.290
A lot less. You'll have
to ask them that question.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.040
Zipline's first delivery drones
were all about getting supplies.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.830
To places with enough space to drop
packages from midair to the ground.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.690
The company's Platform 2 is all about
precision and reaching more dense urban

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:06.523
areas.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:10.340
One of the most important things about
making the overall system work is how do

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.310
we integrate with all the different
companies that want to take advantage of

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:17.660
this to essentially enable
teleportation from their businesses or

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.540
warehouses or stores directly to a home.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:24.410
Zipline can basically show up and
install a magical portal in your wall.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.000
That's kind of how we talk about it. It's.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:26.833
A Harry Potter door.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.690
Yeah. Or I think of like
a Rick and Morty door.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:31.490
You can basically choose
what you want to load.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.080
Do you have any preferences? I mean,
pizza and coffee. Pizza and coffee.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:35.780
Everyone.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:36.200
Has to have.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.033
Pizza and coffee.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:37.790
So.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:39.650
I'll put the pizza in here
and you can do the coffee.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.800
OK. I mean, is this going
to spill in there? OK.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:45.263
Alright.

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:49.910
There you go. So you just
teleported something via Zipline.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:51.560
That's it. Pretty.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:52.220
Cool.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:56.510
We need to be able to deliver silently
with dinner plate level accuracy.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.300
This is the only thing that's coming
close to you, your family, your house.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:05.330
It's really cute. It's not making
very much noise. It's totally safe.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.680
What's going to happen to the
UPS guy? I mean, what about.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.340
Jobs?

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.480
Any new technology,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.330
there's always this worry that it's
going to put people out of jobs. So,

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:18.620
I think the reality is we are in a
world where unemployment is the lowest

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:21.050
basically it has been over
the last 30 years. Generally,

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.340
I think there are not enough humans to
do the work and labor that we want done

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.460
to live abundant, rich lives.

